{{!-- Faith Received — the Notebook workspace.

     Every passage the reader has kept, across every work, with its
     citation, its own note, and a link back to the exact block it came
     from — and the collections those passages have been gathered into.
     Bound by assets/js/page/faith-notebook.js.

     ONE NOTEBOOK, TWO VIEWS. The notes are made in the reader:
     assets/js/faith-reader-tools.js puts a "Save to notebook" popover
     over any selection and keeps a slide-out panel scoped to the work
     in front of you. This is the same data seen whole. Both read and
     write through window.MOFaithNotebook
     (assets/js/lib/faith-notebook-store.js) and neither touches
     localStorage directly, so there is exactly one definition of the
     stored format. Do not add a second one here.

     ── COLLECTIONS LIVE HERE ────────────────────────────────────────

     They used to be a page of their own, /the-faith-received/pins/,
     with its own tab in the Research strip. They are not, because they
     never were a second place to put things: a collection has only ever
     held ENTRY IDS into this same notebook (see
     assets/js/lib/faith-collections-store.js, whose header explains why
     it refuses to hold the records themselves). Two sibling tabs over
     one store presented that as two destinations, and the tell is that
     anything kept while reading appeared in the other tab under "Not in
     a collection" without the reader having done anything. It was one
     panel with a filter on it.

     SO THE COLLECTION STRIP IS A FILTER, and everything below it is the
     Notebook it always was. "Everything" is selected by default and the
     panel is then exactly what it was before collections arrived. Choose
     a collection and the list narrows to it, and the apparatus that only
     makes sense inside a collection appears with it: its working notes,
     its own order, the relations drawn between its members, and an
     export of that collection rather than of the whole notebook.

     A filter, rather than sections in one long list, because almost
     everything a collection carries is collection-scoped and has
     nowhere to live in a flat list — a memo, a hand-made order, edges
     between two members, a Markdown export with a title. Selecting the
     collection gives every one of those a place to be.

     BOOKMARKS STAYS ITS OWN TAB. It answers a different question and is
     a different pair of stores: which WORK you saved (the mo-kit
     Worker's KV) and where in it you had got to (fr_positions, this
     browser). "Where am I in Augustine" is not "what have I gathered
     about grace".

     SELF-CONTAINED. Like _bookmarks-panel.hbs and unlike _ask-panel.hbs,
     this partial ships its own <script> tags at the bottom, so it can be
     dropped into a workspace shell whose template is owned by someone
     else. A host page that also loads any of them is harmless: the
     stores only redefine their window.MOFaith* globals, and
     faith-notebook.js binds once and returns on a second run (it marks
     [data-fn-root] with data-fn-bound). The notebook store must load
     FIRST: the collections store reads MOFaithNotebook.RELATIONS on its
     own first pass, and page-template scripts run before site.min.js so
     nothing bundled can be assumed here.

     Nothing in this panel touches the network, so unlike Compare and the
     Desk it has no reason to wait for its tab and does not.

     DOM contract faith-notebook.js depends on — re-read that file
     before touching any of this:
       - [data-fn-root] wrapping everything. The script marks it bound
         and hangs the focusout handler that cancels a pending Remove.
       - [data-fn-count] — "12 passages", or "3 of 12 passages" while a
         filter is on. Hidden until there is something to count.
       - [data-fn-tools] holding [data-fn-filter] (a search input with a
         real <label>, never placeholder-only) and [data-fn-group]
         (class .notebook-groupby) with three button[data-fn-group-by]:
         "recent", "work" and "author". The third ships hidden and is
         offered only inside a real collection; the first is RELABELLED
         to "Your order" there, because inside a collection the list is
         already in the order the reader put it in. Hidden by the script
         below two passages.
       - [data-fn-collections] — the collection strip, written by the
         script. [data-fn-cbar] holding [data-fn-c-new],
         [data-fn-c-rename], [data-fn-c-delete], [data-fn-c-link] and
         [data-fn-rel] (the relation vocabulary).
       - [data-fn-name-form] > [data-fn-name-legend],
         [data-fn-name-input], [data-fn-name-cancel]
       - [data-fn-memo] wrapping a textarea. Shown only inside a real
         collection.
       - [data-fn-status] — the one status line.
       - [data-fn-list] — everything rendered. Entries carry
         [data-fn-id], a textarea[data-fn-note], and their controls:
         a link, button[data-fn-copy-one], button[data-fn-remove], and
         inside a collection button[data-fn-up] / [data-fn-down] /
         [data-fn-unfile], or outside one a select[data-fn-file].
       - [data-fn-constellation] — the relations drawn in this
         collection, as a ring and as a list.
       - [data-fn-foot] holding [data-fn-copy], [data-fn-download] and
         [data-fn-share], plus [data-fn-foot-note]. Hidden while there is
         nothing to export.

     REMOVE IS TWO PRESSES, both for a passage and for a collection. A
     note is someone's own work, there is no undo, and it cannot be got
     back by clicking anything: it would have to be found and
     re-selected in the text. The reader's panel gets away with one
     click because it lists a handful of passages from the work on
     screen; this lists the whole notebook and the rows all look alike.
     Taking a passage OUT OF a collection is one press, because that one
     is reversible: the passage stays in the notebook and can be filed
     again.

     DRAWING a relation is offered only inside a real collection, which
     is the only scope the store can hold one in — edges belong to a
     collection and both ends must be in it. Relations already drawn are
     SHOWN on every entry everywhere, because hiding them would make a
     passage look like it stood alone. --}}
<div class="notebook-workspace" data-fn-root>
  <div class="notebook-workspace-inner">

    <header class="notebook-head">
      <div class="notebook-head-text">
        <h2 class="notebook-title">Notebook</h2>
        <p class="notebook-count" data-fn-count hidden></p>
      </div>

      <div class="notebook-tools" data-fn-tools hidden>
        <div class="notebook-filter-field">
          <label class="visually-hidden" for="notebook-filter">Filter your notes</label>
          <input
            id="notebook-filter"
            class="notebook-filter"
            type="search"
            data-fn-filter
            maxlength="120"
            autocomplete="off"
            placeholder="Filter your notes&hellip;"
          />
        </div>
        {{!-- .notebook-groupby, not .notebook-group: .notebook-group is
              the class on a rendered "By work" SECTION in the list
              below, and two different things under one class name is
              how a stylesheet starts fighting itself. --}}
        <div class="notebook-groupby" data-fn-group role="group" aria-label="Order your notes">
          <button type="button" class="notebook-groupby-btn is-active" data-fn-group-by="recent" aria-pressed="true">Recent</button>
          <button type="button" class="notebook-groupby-btn" data-fn-group-by="work" aria-pressed="false">By work</button>
          <button type="button" class="notebook-groupby-btn" data-fn-group-by="author" aria-pressed="false" hidden>By author</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    {{!-- ── Collections ──────────────────────────────────────────
          The strip is written by the script: "Everything" first, then
          each collection with its count, then "Not in a collection"
          when something is unfiled AND something else is filed, since
          otherwise that group is just Everything under a second name.
          Empty in the markup rather than seeded with a placeholder,
          because a strip that shows one wrong tab before the script
          runs is worse than a strip that shows none. --}}
    <div class="pins-tabs" data-fn-collections role="tablist" aria-label="Your collections"></div>

    <div class="pins-bar" data-fn-cbar hidden>
      <button type="button" class="pins-act" data-fn-c-new>New collection</button>
      <button type="button" class="pins-act" data-fn-c-rename hidden>Rename</button>
      <button type="button" class="pins-act" data-fn-c-link aria-pressed="false" hidden>Draw a relation</button>
      <label class="pins-rel-field" data-fn-rel-field hidden>
        <span class="visually-hidden">How the first passage relates to the second</span>
        <select class="pins-rel" data-fn-rel>
          <option value="parallels">parallels</option>
          <option value="supports">supports</option>
          <option value="contests">contests</option>
          <option value="cites">cites</option>
          <option value="expands">expands</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <button type="button" class="pins-act pins-act-danger" data-fn-c-delete hidden>Delete collection</button>
    </div>

    {{!-- A real form rather than window.prompt(), which the page this
          was ported from used: prompt cannot be styled, is invisible to
          the flow of the page for a screen reader, and is blocked
          outright in some embedded browsers, where the button would
          simply appear to do nothing. --}}
    <form class="pins-name-form" data-fn-name-form hidden>
      <label class="pins-name-label">
        <span data-fn-name-legend>Name the new collection</span>
        <input type="text" class="pins-name-input" data-fn-name-input maxlength="60" autocomplete="off" />
      </label>
      <button type="submit" class="pins-act pins-act-primary">Save</button>
      <button type="button" class="pins-act" data-fn-name-cancel>Cancel</button>
    </form>

    <p class="notebook-status" data-fn-status role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></p>

    {{!-- Working notes for the collection as a whole, distinct from the
          note on any one passage. Saved on blur: this writes the whole
          store back, and doing that per keystroke is a stutter on a
          phone. --}}
    <div class="pins-memo" data-fn-memo hidden>
      <label class="pins-memo-label" for="notebook-memo">Working notes for this collection</label>
      <textarea id="notebook-memo" class="pins-memo-text" rows="3" placeholder="What you are assembling here, and why&hellip;"></textarea>
    </div>

    <div class="notebook-list" data-fn-list></div>

    <div class="pins-constellation" data-fn-constellation></div>

    <div class="notebook-foot" data-fn-foot hidden>
      <div class="notebook-foot-acts">
        <button type="button" class="notebook-act" data-fn-copy>Copy all</button>
        <button type="button" class="notebook-act" data-fn-download>Download</button>
        <button type="button" class="notebook-act" data-fn-share>Share as a link</button>
      </div>
      {{!-- Said plainly, and said here rather than in a tooltip. The
            notebook is in this browser, so it does not follow the
            reader to a phone and clearing site data clears it. The
            script adds a second sentence inside a collection, about
            what a share link does and does not carry. --}}
      <p class="notebook-foot-note" data-fn-foot-note>Kept in this browser only. Copy or download anything you want to keep.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</div>

{{!-- Last inside the panel on purpose: a non-visual node placed first
      would take the :first-child margin reset off the header above it.
      The notebook store FIRST, always: faith-notebook.js reads
      window.MOFaithNotebook on its first line and the collections store
      reads MOFaithNotebook.RELATIONS on its own, and page-template
      scripts run before site.min.js, so nothing bundled can be assumed
      here. --}}
<script src="{{asset "js/lib/faith-notebook-store.js"}}"></script>
<script src="{{asset "js/lib/faith-collections-store.js"}}"></script>
<script src="{{asset "js/page/faith-notebook.js"}}"></script>
